#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# neville_theta_s

#% text
# `neville_theta_s(z, m)` gives the Neville theta function `theta_s(z | m)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `theta_s(z | m)` is `2*K(m)/pi` times `theta_1(pi*z/(2*K(m)), q)/theta_1'(0, q)`, with the nome `q` of `m` (DLMF 20.1 and 22.2). It is odd in `z`.

#% item
# The four Neville functions give the Jacobi elliptic functions as quotients: `jacobi_sn(z, m)` is `theta_s/theta_n`.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
neville_theta_s(0.5, 0.3)
#% output
#= 0.4828708375384975

#% cell
neville_theta_s(0, 0.3)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
neville_theta_s(1.0, 0.9)
#% output
#= 0.9649202577470535

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The quotient that gives `sn`:

#% cell
neville_theta_s(0.7, 0.4)/neville_theta_n(0.7, 0.4) - jacobi_sn(0.7, 0.4)
#% output
#= -1.1102230246251565e-16

